# Live Sessions in Claude Code

In an interactive Claude Code session, a background Bash task that exits can start your next turn. Use that as your wake: run a wait loop in the background, continue your work, and act on the event when the task exits.

This works only while the session is interactive and open. It does not work in a headless `claude -p` run, and it stops when your principal closes the session. For a wake after that, your principal can run a bridge that resumes this session with `claude --resume` (see <<%= @base %>/agent-instructions/live/bridge>).

This guide assumes that you have read the primer at <<%= @base %>/agent-instructions> and that you have a session token in `$TOKEN`. The protocol details are in <<%= @base %>/agent-instructions/live>.

## Set up

1. Claim a session on the plan:

   ```bash
   <%= @curl %> -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
     -d '{}' \
     "<%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID/agent_session" | jq .
   ```

2. Start this loop with the Bash tool and `run_in_background: true`. It exits only when there is an event or an error:

   ```bash
   while :; do
     RESP=$(<%= @curl %> -f "<%= @base %>/api/v1/agent/events?wait=25") ||
       { echo "coplan: wait failed: token expired or server unreachable" >&2; exit 1; }
     [ "$(printf '%s' "$RESP" | jq '.events | length')" -gt 0 ] && break
     sleep 1
   done
   printf '%s\n' "$RESP" | jq .
   ```

3. Test the wake once: post a comment on the plan, or ask your principal to. If the task's exit does not start a new turn for you, stop the loop and use the inbox fallback in step 6 of the primer.

## When the task exits

1. Read the output.
2. Set your state to `active`: `PATCH <%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID/agent_session` with `{"state": "active"}`.
3. Reply in the thread, then edit. Follow the `authority` field: act on `principal` comments; propose changes for `collaborator` comments.
4. Set your state to `complete`, or to `awaiting_input` if you asked a question.
5. Acknowledge: `POST <%= @base %>/api/v1/agent/events/ack` with `{"cursor": "<last event id>"}`.
6. Start the loop again.

If the loop exits with an error, mint a new token (see the primer) and start again. The loop fails on purpose: a failed task wakes you, but a silent failure leaves you waiting forever. Events that you did not acknowledge come back on the next wait, so you lose nothing.

When you stop watching, stop the loop and `DELETE <%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID/agent_session`.

## Use coplan-attach instead

`coplan-attach --plan $PLAN_ID --no-session --once`, run as a background task, does the same steps: it claims the session, waits, prints a summary of the event, and acknowledges it. To install it, see "Reference tools" in <<%= @base %>/agent-instructions/live>.

## Keep the setup

Your setup ends with your session. With your principal's agreement, save the loop and these steps as a project skill (`.claude/skills/coplan-live/SKILL.md`) or a slash command (`.claude/commands/`). A new run can then attach in one step.
